=======================================================================
DeepSeek Harness (dsh) has been installed.

QUICK START — CLI
    dsh --help
    dsh web                      # serves the web UI on 127.0.0.1:3080
                                 # (prints a one-time token URL)

WEB UI ACCESS — READ THIS FIRST
    The harness only trusts loopback requests. Open the UI as
        http://127.0.0.1:3080/?token=<token>
    or from your workstation through an SSH tunnel:
        ssh -L 3080:127.0.0.1:3080 <user>@<this-host>
        then http://localhost:3080
    Binding the UI to a LAN address, or putting it behind nginx/Caddy,
    will NOT work for host-side file operations (the trust fence rejects
    non-loopback Host headers) — this is a security design, not a bug.

RUN AS A BOOT-TIME SERVICE
    sysrc dsh_web_enable=YES
    service dsh_web start        # prints the token URL
    # State (config, sessions, tokens) lives in /var/db/dsh; point it
    # somewhere else with `sysrc dsh_web_chdir=/path`, which also becomes
    # the default cwd for agent commands. Other knobs: dsh_web_user,
    # dsh_web_host, dsh_web_port, dsh_web_env, dsh_web_env_file.

AGENT SANDBOX (FreeBSD jail backend)
    A setuid helper is installed at
    %%PREFIX%%/libexec/dsh-jail-run — it confines every agent command in
    an ephemeral jail. Keep it root-owned and setuid; do not move or
    chown it. If your site policy forbids setuid binaries, remove the
    setuid bit: the harness then falls back to danger-full-access mode
    (documented in FREEBSD.md in the fork repository).

RUNTIME DEPENDENCIES (installed automatically)
    node24, bash, ripgrep. bash is required — the terminal tool drives a
    persistent bash PTY session. ripgrep powers the grep/glob tools.

DOCS
    Upstream:  https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness
    Fork (FreeBSD notes, FREEBSD.md):
               https://github.com/skywalk163/deepseek-harness
=======================================================================
